title: Checking and reindexing a repository
tags: Storage
kind: guide
summary: mf repo check compares what the store derives with a rebuild; mf repo reindex derives it again.

```
mf -n music repo check       # what no longer holds together; exit code 1 on a problem
mf -n music repo reindex     # derive again what the store derives
```

`check` compares the store's derived key spaces — ids, sets, partitions, the
forest, trigrams, postings — with a rebuild from the primary data, and lists the
differences, one line each (nothing when the store is healthy; a hundred lines at
most). It also names the
metarecords an older daemon let hold several positions in one forest: the data is
not damaged, but the daemon no longer means anything by it ([[One position per forest]]).

`reindex` rebuilds all the derived data from the primary data. Derived data is a
function of the primary data, so reindexing never loses anything; it is the repair
for what `check` reports.

The comparison holds neither side in memory: the derived data of a large
repository — every trigram of every text, per holder — is larger than the machine
that checks it, and the check also runs inside the daemon, on each automatic backup
([[Backups and restore]]). Both sides are streamed into fingerprints (a sum of entry
hashes per key space, cut in buckets), and only the buckets whose fingerprints differ
are read again to name their entries. What stays resident is a parent per forest
node. A check that listed everything it compared was killed for lack of memory on a
3 GB store, taking the daemon with it at every start.

Both hold the repository while they run: other work on it waits.
